            <folder name="II.1.1.2.3 [CT2] Managed Entity &amp; Relationship" id="id-e71a424cbe70428192dacc7dcc9915dc">
              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.1.2.3. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Canonical element directory: canonicalId=910634fb1621416c818a022ab0b5d3a1; Exchange identifier=id-d53727d908365757a293b0896b2c769b. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Managed Entity &amp; Relationship" id="id-d53727d908365757a293b0896b2c769b">
                <documentation>- **Architectural Scope**: Identify, classify, relate, and maintain the structural integrity of managed healthcare and administrative entities throughout the enterprise landscape.
- **Functional Responsibilities**:
  - Uniquely identifying core domain entities (Persons, Practitioners, Roles, Organisations, Locations, Services, Devices).
  - Resolving and maintaining multi-authority identifier systems and aliases without destructive overwriting.
  - Representing and traversing multi-dimensional relationships (affiliations, organizational hierarchies, physical location trees, care team memberships).
  - Maintaining referential integrity across interconnected entity graphs.
- **Anti-Responsibilities**: Does not own physical database relational tables or foreign key constraints (Domain 04/07).

This reusable capability keeps managed healthcare entities identifiable and their relationships structurally intelligible. It supplies common identity, alias and relationship behaviour recurring in persons, practitioners, organisations, locations and devices. Reuse concerns those architectural responsibilities, allowing the healthcare-specific meaning of each entity to remain with its governing context.

**Boundary**

Relationship integrity is not a physical database design. Correlating entities does not establish originating clinical identity authority or a new standalone Correlation capability.</documentation>
