            <folder name="II.1.1.2.5 [CT2] Search &amp; Discovery" id="id-78023e9850eb424da7d67dc21bcf0b79">
              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.1.2.5. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Canonical element directory: canonicalId=6a45701634f04b43919d9152a61e5f2e; Exchange identifier=id-e11f02764fe55ff29c0782db21198040. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Search &amp; Discovery" id="id-e11f02764fe55ff29c0782db21198040">
                <documentation>- **Architectural Scope**: Index, search, resolve, filter, and discover information assets, entities, directory endpoints, and healthcare services across the enterprise.
- **Functional Responsibilities**:
  - Providing multi-criteria indexed search across complex entity graphs (e.g., find active cardiologists at Location X accepting new referrals).
  - Resolving service and practitioner communication endpoints for addressed delivery.
  - Supporting bounded, paginated clinical queries across longitudinal patient records.
  - Applying dynamic security and consent filters to search results prior to egress.
- **Anti-Responsibilities**: Does not dictate specific search engine indexes, inverted index implementations, or SQL query syntaxes.

Search &amp; Discovery makes information, entities and service endpoints findable through governed criteria and bounded queries. It addresses the recurring need to locate relevant material or a destination before it can be used. Security-qualified results preserve the distinction between knowing that something exists and being entitled to obtain or disclose it.

**Boundary**

Discovery does not decide policy or prescribe a search engine, index format or query language.</documentation>
