          <folder name="II.1.1.3 [CT1] Trust, Accountability &amp; Assurance" id="id-6547ec3055ff4a288a1d619778bcadf1">
            <folder name="II.1.1.3.1 [CT2] Information Authority Qualification" id="id-ed6c786de09346c0b28cc70d07a8a83e">
              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.1.3.1. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Canonical element directory: canonicalId=cbba9984dd404deabed41fdcc96088b1; Exchange identifier=id-e4ba93a12970559e916ecaabb0eb0bb3. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Information Authority Qualification" id="id-e4ba93a12970559e916ecaabb0eb0bb3">
                <documentation>Represent and qualify the source-authority and credibility standing of information independently of transport, persistence and concurrency.

**Overview**

Identity correlation, clinical-history synthesis, reference-content use and assurance evidence all need to distinguish the standing of an assertion from technical acceptance. This recurring ability is insufficiently explicit in the existing fourteen EC definitions and is proposed as a new peer responsibility.

**Intent**

Keep source credibility explicit even when the technical handling of competing assertions is equally valid.

**Boundary**

This is not independent service assurance, a universal conflict arbiter or clinical truth determination. Existing EC-13 governs authority nomenclature; this capability applies and preserves the qualified standing.</documentation>
