            <folder name="II.1.2.14.2 [CT2] Clinical Intervention" id="id-71c2463436444dd49e04b977f825f60b">
              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.2.14.2. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Historical predecessor: R.2.34 [CT2] Clinical Intervention; canonicalId=958345ee65f446d3b1259b8895ab100f; Exchange identifier=id-88c88cae931a551b930f455b82c63aa5 Canonical element directory: canonicalId=5359d1b0ff814a0e871384469f5ebcd7; Exchange identifier=id-06c36cc9da8c5ad0b22ec96ff99f45aa. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Clinical Intervention" id="id-06c36cc9da8c5ad0b22ec96ff99f45aa">
                <documentation>The capability to perform clinical actions intended to alter, maintain or otherwise manage an individual's health state in accordance with an established care intent.

Clinical Intervention is the ability to perform a clinical action under an established care intent. It marks the transition from intended care to care actually performed, whether the action seeks to alter, maintain or otherwise manage health. Its exercise can establish that treatment occurred even when the desired clinical result was not achieved.

**Intent**

Put the intended clinical response into practice through appropriately authorised care.

**Boundary**

Orders and appointments express or arrange future activity; they are not evidence that the intervention occurred. Therapy, medication and procedural examples do not constitute an approved three-child taxonomy: that proposed decomposition remains provisional.

## Qualified relationship assertions

Original source predicates, evidence and qualifications remain in the metadata. The ARB has now adjudicated their native treatment; explicitly unestablished relationship existence remains excluded. Native representation does not promote source standing.

| Source predicate | Target element | Source standing / currentness | ARB native treatment |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| enabled_by | [[CT2] Health Information Access](cc253aea3c5f489da0502df74dde1669.md) | established / current | Serving (reverse) — `id-bb561847ed825813ada60492cd071523` |

