            <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.2.15. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Historical predecessor: R.2.49 [CT1] Population Health &amp; Health-System Planning; canonicalId=a5003436a7f348b1bff60ba593adfa27; Exchange identifier=id-f8a453be4c9450b9958ae54ca6630f30 Canonical element directory: canonicalId=c63b38b37e9b4e9b97dc87ed54604def; Exchange identifier=id-3dc006e536ce50b3acdc8f625eccb36d. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
            <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT1] Population Health &amp; Health-System Planning" id="id-3dc006e536ce50b3acdc8f625eccb36d">
              <documentation>Understand population health through assessment/surveillance and applicable event/notification management; assess health-service demand and plan required capacity and its distribution.

This capability connects an understanding of population health with consideration of future health-service needs. Surveillance addresses population state, significant events and notification; planning assesses service demand and required capacity and distribution. Population evidence can inform planning without predetermining its conclusions or becoming the operational allocation of resources.

**Intent**

Support informed public-health awareness and a reasoned view of the services a population will require.

**Boundary**

Population conclusions differ from individual clinical findings, and planned capacity differs from capacity currently usable by a service.

**Harmonia Architectural Relevance**

Governed population information and formal notifications materially support health activity. Consuming planning context does not make the platform the health-system planner or owner of its resources.

## ARB identity continuity

Historical identity [a5003436a7f348b1bff60ba593adfa27](a5003436a7f348b1bff60ba593adfa27.md) has direct correspondence with this capability under HCRL 1.1.5-002. Historical provenance and UUID reservation remain with that record; this resource retains its existing identity and independent standing.</documentation>
