              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.3.4.7. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Historical predecessor: R.2.113 [CT2] Provider Administration; canonicalId=a352fd50419d451887c85fcb98566cd5; Exchange identifier=id-b12071ab88dc573b9ab955165b063211 Canonical element directory: canonicalId=d6312f85968a4209bafff5ed009297a6; Exchange identifier=id-7b438cff4f2d5b6d928cd7ea538f67a4. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Provider Administration" id="id-7b438cff4f2d5b6d928cd7ea538f67a4">
                <documentation>Provider Administration maintains the practitioner, role, affiliation and engagement context needed to interpret healthcare participation. National identifiers, professional profiles, role-specific endpoints and privilege records support different questions: who the practitioner is, in what capacity they act and how they can be reached. Directory representation makes those distinctions usable without replacing the business governance that establishes professional standing.

**Overview**

Provider Administration maintains the practitioner, role, affiliation and engagement context needed to interpret healthcare participation. National identifiers, professional profiles, role-specific endpoints and privilege records support different questions: who the practitioner is, in what capacity they act and how they can be reached. Directory representation makes those distinctions usable without replacing the business governance that establishes professional standing.

**Intent**

Provider Administration maintains the practitioner, role, affiliation and engagement context needed to interpret healthcare participation. National identifiers, professional profiles, role-specific endpoints and privilege records support different questions: who the practitioner is, in what capacity they act and how they can be reached. Directory representation makes those distinctions usable without replacing the business governance that establishes professional standing.

**Boundary**

Information administration does not confer authority over the represented person, organisation, service, place, device or clinical activity.

---

## History

### Intent — predecessor explanation

Provide a trustworthy provider context for routing, participation and the application of relevant qualification and privilege constraints.

### Boundary — predecessor explanation

Practitioner Role Resolution establishes neither Clinical Privilege nor Operational Privilege. The descriptive practitioner and role groupings do not establish reconciled CT ancestry.

## Qualified relationship assertions

Original source predicates, evidence and qualifications remain in the metadata. The ARB has now adjudicated their native treatment; explicitly unestablished relationship existence remains excluded. Native representation does not promote source standing.

