              <documentation>Continuum navigation coordinate: II.1.3.6.21. Navigation asserts no semantic containment. Occurrence: architectural; no additional architectural instance or semantic relationship. Historical predecessor: R.2.152 [CT2] Service Capacity Management; canonicalId=ac205f374c9d41c3b6d18a6b7c356152; Exchange identifier=id-122cf0d3f0055bea8cc85ff72d4d23ee Canonical element directory: canonicalId=d86105bf9c86445394088f65c895d37f; Exchange identifier=id-2de1ef69d9675c59baa5c66e541e1df5. Existing governed hierarchy member; directory retained for leaves.</documentation>
              <element xsi:type="archimate:Capability" name="[CT2] Service Capacity Management" id="id-2de1ef69d9675c59baa5c66e541e1df5">
                <documentation>Service Capacity Management provides a higher-order view by aggregating capacity information across services and facilities. It can consider occupancy, waits and diversion while preserving the service-specific meaning of the underlying capacity. Its purpose is not to erase differences between clinic, ward, theatre, emergency, outpatient and mobile-service operation.

**Overview**

Service Capacity Management provides a higher-order view by aggregating capacity information across services and facilities. It can consider occupancy, waits and diversion while preserving the service-specific meaning of the underlying capacity. Its purpose is not to erase differences between clinic, ward, theatre, emergency, outpatient and mobile-service operation.

**Intent**

Service Capacity Management provides a higher-order view by aggregating capacity information across services and facilities. It can consider occupancy, waits and diversion while preserving the service-specific meaning of the underlying capacity. Its purpose is not to erase differences between clinic, ward, theatre, emergency, outpatient and mobile-service operation.

**Boundary**

Information administration does not confer authority over the represented person, organisation, service, place, device or clinical activity.

---

## History

### Boundary — predecessor explanation

Consuming contextual capacity does not acquire the originating capacity-management responsibility or infer capacity solely from resource counts.

### Harmonia Architectural Relevance — predecessor explanation

Cross-service operational visibility materially supports coordination while source service contexts retain their authority.

