### DesignNote-Digital-Twin-Behaviour-and-Execution-Semantics — original lines 169-176

Source: `docs/markdown/05-information-systems-architecture/design-notes/DesignNote-Digital-Twin-Behaviour-and-Execution-Semantics.md`; U19; source-qualified assertion, current subject standing unchanged.

Interested Behaviours process their respective BehaviourStimulus or
BehaviourEffector **sequentially**, subject to the
[failure dispositions](../../../../docs/markdown/05-information-systems-architecture/design-notes/DesignNote-Digital-Twin-Behaviour-and-Execution-Semantics.md#10-behaviour-failure-dispositions). With no failure,
processing completes after the interested Behaviour sequence completes. Failure
may stop the failing Behaviour or halt the Twin, including immediately interrupting
the current input. The Twin may commence the next queued input only after current
input processing completes and no Twin halt applies. An empty queue or a stopped
Twin does not imply that an input is executing.

### DesignNote-Digital-Twin-Behaviour-and-Execution-Semantics — original lines 200-207

