        <element xsi:type="archimate:Requirement" name="REQ-FND-001: Durable Ingress Acceptance Boundary" id="id-3d54fd52d9c95b22b8ac8790be876b38">
          <documentation>### Normative Requirement Statement
> **Harmonia shall not positively acknowledge acceptance of an incoming event until responsibility for that event has crossed the required durable acceptance boundary.**

### Architectural Intent &amp; Rationale
This requirement eliminates the critical vulnerability identified in [Silent Data Loss via False Acceptance](../../../../docs/markdown/01-motivation/drivers-assessments/assessments.md). When an upstream external system transmits a clinical event (such as an emergency admission, critical lab result, or medication order), it remains the responsibility of that upstream sender until Harmonia has durably secured the event.

Releasing an upstream sender from responsibility (by returning a positive transport acknowledgement) before the event is committed to durable storage creates a vulnerable window where gateway crashes, memory exhaustions, or hardware reboots cause silent, unrecoverable data loss.

### Protocol-Neutral Delineation &amp; Downstream Realisations
- **Normative Rule**: The requirement mandates that the durable acceptance boundary must be crossed before positive acknowledgement. It is completely protocol-neutral.
- **Downstream Realisations**:
  - In HL7 v2 over MLLP integration (owned by Domain 06 Integration Architecture), this rule is realised by forbidding an `MSA-1 = AA` (Application Accept) acknowledgement until downstream persistence or queue publication succeeds. If persistence fails, the gateway MUST return `MSA-1 = AE` (Application Error) to trigger upstream sender retry.
  - In FHIR RESTful APIs, this rule is realised by returning `201 Created` or `200 OK` only after transactional database commitment, returning `202 Accepted` only when durable asynchronous queueing is verified, and returning `500 Internal Server Error` or `503 Service Unavailable` on durable failure.
  - In Ingress Dual-Write safety (`REC-001`), this rule enforces that in-memory active caching alone is insufficient to satisfy positive acceptance.

- **Primary Motivation**: [Silent Data Loss via False Acceptance](../../../../docs/markdown/01-motivation/drivers-assessments/assessments.md), [Continuous Clinical Service Availability](../../../../docs/markdown/01-motivation/drivers-assessments/drivers.md)
- **Governing Principles**: [AX-05 (State Separation)](../principle/0412a2084f0145babc4f022c71fea65f.md), [AX-10 (Failure as Normal Condition)](../principle/0d9673833eb840eab03804aa076c1b0c.md), [AX-15 (Explicit Representation of Uncertainty)](../principle/ef8abbaec37e4890ad78b9f263e67906.md)
- **Historical Identifier Reference**: `REQ-INGRESS-001`</documentation>
          <property key="Baseline / Native Definition ID" value="REQ-FND-001"/>
          <property key="Baseline / Record State" value="current"/>
          <property key="Baseline / Architectural Status" value="Accepted"/>
          <property key="Baseline / Source Path" value="docs/markdown/01-motivation/requirements-constraints/foundational-requirements.md"/>
          <property key="Baseline / Source Section" value="## REQ-FND-001: Durable Ingress Acceptance Boundary"/>
