\cataloguefield{subsection}{Capability Overview}

Digital Accountability \&amp; Trust makes activity attributable and evaluates whether security-relevant activity and information use were appropriate. Attribution records participation together with acting capacity and relevant permission or constraint context; accountability uses that context to consider conduct against policy and obligations. A record of who acted is therefore necessary context, rather than proof that the action was permitted.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Definition / governed semantic detail}

Establish governed attribution and accountability for activity and information use.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Intent}

Support meaningful responsibility for information use and security-relevant conduct.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary — v0.1 explanation}

Authentication, attribution and authorisation must remain distinguishable. Expected downstream evidentiary use does not establish new BC-to-BEC relationships.


