\cataloguefield{subsection}{R1.x / R2.x scope}
\textbf{Not Addressed in R1.x / R2.x}. This is a release-scope annotation, not Candidate status or missing architecture.
\cataloguefield{subsection}{Capability Overview}

Procedure Administration integrates procedural bookings and the documentation of procedures performed. It keeps the scheduled case distinct from operative, anaesthetic and post-procedure information received later. That separation supports continuity across scheduling and clinical documentation without inferring that a booking establishes performance.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Definition — Reviewed candidate}

Procedure Administration integrates procedural bookings and the documentation of procedures performed. It keeps the scheduled case distinct from operative, anaesthetic and post-procedure information received later. That separation supports continuity across scheduling and clinical documentation without inferring that a booking establishes performance.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary — retained v0.1 source explanation}

Administrative integration neither performs the procedure nor originates its professional findings.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Harmonia Architectural Relevance — retained v0.1 source explanation}

Correlated bookings and procedural documents support cross-system care context while the surgical or procedural service retains clinical authority.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Boundary}

Information administration does not confer authority over the represented person, organisation, service, place, device or clinical activity.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Proposed Placement Rationale}

Responsibility-preserving placement under the human-reviewed BEC hierarchy; the structural assertion remains Proposed.


\cataloguefield{subsection}{Reviewed reconciliation provenance}
Reconciliation against the explicit human-reviewed BEC candidate hierarchy. Bounded semantic reconciliation; no unconstrained synthesis \hyperref[evidence:405e9742dcba8b45]{reviewed-bec-hierarchy.json:2}
